        ['q' => 'What is a public adjuster?', 'a' => '<p>A public adjuster is a licensed insurance professional who represents the policyholder, not the insurance company, on a property insurance claim. In Texas, public adjusters are licensed and regulated by the Texas Department of Insurance under <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.4102.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Insurance Code Chapter 4102</a>. They inspect and document damage, review the policy, prepare an estimate, and communicate and negotiate with the insurer.</p>'],
        ['q' => 'How is a public adjuster different from the insurance company\'s adjuster?', 'a' => '<p>The company adjuster, whether staff or independent, is hired by the insurer (or TWIA) and evaluates the claim under the insurer\'s guidelines. A public adjuster works for you under a written contract that must state "We represent the insured only." Read <a href="/are-insurance-adjusters-honest/">are insurance adjusters honest?</a> for a balanced look.</p>'],
        ['q' => 'Is a public adjuster the same as an attorney?', 'a' => '<p>No. Public adjusters do not give legal advice, file lawsuits, or represent anyone in court. For questions about suits, bad faith, or legal deadlines, consult a Texas attorney.</p>'],
